WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.750
Asha taps Place Order.

00:00:02.625 --> 00:00:03.625
Checkout stalls.

00:00:04.488 --> 00:00:07.112
She cannot tell whether payment succeeded.

00:00:08.012 --> 00:00:11.025
A slow request alone does not prove garbage

00:00:11.038 --> 00:00:12.188
collection caused it.

00:00:13.833 --> 00:00:15.796
The Java heap holds objects.

00:00:16.658 --> 00:00:19.846
Garbage collection reclaims unreachable objects.

00:00:20.721 --> 00:00:24.646
Some phases pause application threads; other phases run

00:00:24.721 --> 00:00:25.821
alongside them.

00:00:27.433 --> 00:00:29.983
This example request takes two seconds.

00:00:30.858 --> 00:00:34.458
An overlapping pause lasts three hundred milliseconds.

00:00:35.333 --> 00:00:37.096
The bank also takes time.

00:00:37.958 --> 00:00:40.883
Do not blame the entire delay on garbage

00:00:40.883 --> 00:00:41.433
collection.

00:00:43.033 --> 00:00:45.946
Match request traces and pause logs from the

00:00:45.946 --> 00:00:46.771
same server.

00:00:47.646 --> 00:00:51.683
Check payment waits, database spans, and CPU before

00:00:51.683 --> 00:00:53.171
deciding what to fix.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.688
Use Java Flight Recorder to find allocation hotspots.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:03.050
Remove redundant work without changing order totals.

00:01:03.925 --> 00:01:07.075
Compare the same workload before choosing another

00:01:07.075 --> 00:01:07.588
collector.

00:01:09.167 --> 00:01:13.292
Validate tail latency, errors, memory, and CPU.

00:01:14.217 --> 00:01:18.329
Keep the original payment identity and reconcile uncertain

00:01:18.329 --> 00:01:18.992
outcomes.

00:01:19.867 --> 00:01:22.579
A timeout is not proof of failed payment.
